{"entryType": "UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)", "primaryAccession": "P35561", "uniProtkbId": "KCNJ2_MOUSE", "entryAudit": {"firstPublicDate": "1994-06-01", "lastAnnotationUpdateDate": "2026-06-10", "lastSequenceUpdateDate": "1994-06-01", "entryVersion": 212, "sequenceVersion": 1}, "annotationScore": 5.0, "organism": {"scientificName": "Mus musculus", "commonName": "Mouse", "taxonId": 10090, "lineage": ["Eukaryota", "Metazoa", "Chordata", "Craniata", "Vertebrata", "Euteleostomi", "Mammalia", "Eutheria", "Euarchontoglires", "Glires", "Rodentia", "Myomorpha", "Muroidea", "Muridae", "Murinae", "Mus", "Mus"]}, "proteinExistence": "1: Evidence at protein level", "proteinDescription": {"recommendedName": {"fullName": {"value": "Inward rectifier potassium channel 2"}}, "alternativeNames": [{"fullName": {"value": "Inward rectifier K(+) channel Kir2.1"}, "shortNames": [{"value": "IRK-1"}]}, {"fullName": {"value": "Potassium channel, inwardly rectifying subfamily J member 2"}}]}, "genes": [{"geneName": {"value": "Kcnj2"}, "synonyms": [{"value": "Irk1"}]}], "comments": [{"texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "source": "UniProtKB", "id": "P63252"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269", "source": "PubMed", "id": "7680768"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269", "source": "PubMed", "id": "8282096"}], "value": "Inward rectifier potassium channels are characterized by a greater tendency to allow potassium to flow into the cell rather than out of it. Their voltage dependence is regulated by the concentration of extracellular potassium; as external potassium is raised, the voltage range of the channel opening shifts to more positive voltages. The inward rectification is mainly due to the blockage of outward current by internal magnesium (PubMed:7680768, PubMed:8282096). Can be blocked by extracellular barium and cesium. Probably participates in establishing action potential waveform and excitability of neuronal and muscle tissues (By similarity)"}], "commentType": "FUNCTION"}, {"commentType": "CATALYTIC ACTIVITY", "reaction": {"name": "K(+)(in) = K(+)(out)", "reactionCrossReferences": [{"database": "Rhea", "id": "RHEA:29463"}, {"database": "ChEBI", "id": "CHEBI:29103"}], "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269", "source": "PubMed", "id": "7680768"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269", "source": "PubMed", "id": "8282096"}]}}, {"texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "source": "UniProtKB", "id": "Q64273"}], "value": "Activated by phosphatidylinositol 4,5 biphosphate (PtdIns(4,5)P2)"}], "commentType": "ACTIVITY REGULATION"}, {"texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "source": "UniProtKB", "id": "P63252"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269", "source": "PubMed", "id": "21209095"}], "value": "Homotetramer. Homomultimeric and heteromultimeric association with KCNJ4/Kir2.3. Can form heteromeric channels with Kir2.6/KCNJ18 (PubMed:21209095). Associates, via its PDZ-recognition domain, with a complex containing LIN7A, LIN7B, LIN7C, DLG1, CASK and APBA1"}], "commentType": "SUBUNIT"}, {"commentType": "INTERACTION", "interactions": [{"interactantOne": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P35561", "intActId": "EBI-703793"}, "interactantTwo": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P35561", "geneName": "Kcnj2", "intActId": "EBI-703793"}, "numberOfExperiments": 7, "organismDiffer": false}, {"interactantOne": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P35561", "intActId": "EBI-703793"}, "interactantTwo": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P24588", "geneName": "AKAP5", "intActId": "EBI-703640"}, "numberOfExperiments": 2, "organismDiffer": true}, {"interactantOne": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P35561", "intActId": "EBI-703793"}, "interactantTwo": {"uniProtKBAccession": "B2RYN6", "geneName": "Ap1g1", "intActId": "EBI-4319005"}, "numberOfExperiments": 2, "organismDiffer": true}, {"interactantOne": {"uniProtKBAccession": "P35561", "intActId": "EBI-703793"}, "interactantTwo": {"uniProtKBAccession": "Q15700-4", "geneName": "DLG2", "intActId": "EBI-663057"}, "numberOfExperiments": 6, "organismDif